# SYNTHESISED to exercise a lane holding far more calls than the bar drawn
# for it has columns: sixty aws calls of 40ms each, one every three seconds
# from 3s to 180s. None of them overlaps another, so PackLanes puts all
# sixty in ONE lane, and at every width this interface renders at there are
# fewer columns in the bar than there are calls in the lane -- with the
# calls spaced closely enough that every column holds at least one. Drawing
# each column as merely lit or unlit therefore paints the whole lane solid
# while its true occupancy is 40ms in every three seconds, about 1.3%:
# waiting rendered as work, which is the inversion of the one question this
# view exists to answer. The bar's shading is what keeps that visible. The
# line shape is testdata/two-providers.log's "Received downstream response"
# verbatim. The first line is not itself a response: it exists only to
# anchor the scanner's base clock a few seconds before the first real span,
# so that span's start is not clamped to zero by ReportedBuilder (see the
# doc comment on span.Span). Values are invented; no line here is from a
# real run.
